(* README *)

1 - Summary
    -------

    CARRYLESS SEQUENT is A002's certified checker for closed implicational
    Hilbert sequents with axiom schemas K and S and modus ponens.

    The principal verifier operates on inductive normal forms rather than on
    nested arithmetic container codes:

      normalized_stepb  : list NormalizedFormula ->
                          NormalizedRule -> NormalizedFormula -> bool

      normalized_linesb : list NormalizedFormula ->
                          NormalizedDerivation -> bool

      normalized_verifyb : NormalizedDerivation ->
                           NormalizedFormula -> bool

    Three closed reflection theorems identify executable acceptance with the
    independent inductive validity judgments:

      normalized_stepb_iff :
        normalized_stepb prefix rule formula = true <->
        NormalizedStep prefix rule formula

      normalized_linesb_iff :
        normalized_linesb prefix lines = true <->
        NormalizedLines prefix lines

      normalized_verifyb_iff :
        normalized_verifyb lines target = true <->
        NormalizedProof lines target

    A002 exposes a certified A001-coded arithmetic entry point:

      A002_Verify_certified : nat -> nat -> nat

    It normalizes the arithmetic derivation and target, requires
    target-sensitive normalized validity, and returns a canonical payload
    binding the accepted derivation code to its target code.

    Certified rejection diagnostics distinguish the failed boundary:

      derivation normalization
        STAGE_DERIVATION_HEADER, index 0,
        ERR_NONCANONICAL_DERIVATION

      target normalization
        STAGE_FORMULA, index 0,
        ERR_NONCANONICAL_FORMULA

      normalized K/S/MP obligation
        STAGE_RULE, first failing line index,
        ERR_NORMALIZED_STEP_REJECTED

      empty normalized derivation
        STAGE_CONCLUSION, index 0,
        ERR_EMPTY_DERIVATION

      requested conclusion mismatch
        STAGE_CONCLUSION, final line index,
        ERR_BAD_CONCLUSION

    Arithmetic inputs are guarded against non-canonical A001 pair codes before
    their decoded components are interpreted as lists, formulas, lines, rules,
    or certificates.


2 - State
    -----

                      ┌──────────────────────────────────┐
                      │                  x               │
                      │   └─┴─┴─┴─┴─┴─┴─┴─┴─┴─┘ 8 / 10   │
                      └──────────────────────────────────┘

    Evaluation: 8, Decent. The normalized checker is effective, structurally
    recursive, documented, extracted, and proved equivalent to inductive K/S/MP
    validity. The arithmetic compatibility verifier remains executable and
    deterministically rejects malformed inputs.

    The principal reflection theorems and the published arithmetic agreement
    facts are closed under the global context. The package is not yet fully
    peer reviewed.

    Certified payload checking is independent of the verifier. It validates
    canonical payload structure, exact derivation/target binding, arithmetic
    normalization, and target-sensitive inductive proof validity.


3 - Intuition
    ---------

    A proof is checked most directly as typed finite syntax:

      Inductive NormalizedFormula :=
      | NFVar : nat -> NormalizedFormula
      | NFImp : NormalizedFormula -> NormalizedFormula -> NormalizedFormula.

      Inductive NormalizedRule :=
      | NRAxK
      | NRAxS
      | NRMP : nat -> nat -> NormalizedRule.

      Record NormalizedLine := {
        normalized_line_rule    : NormalizedRule;
        normalized_line_formula : NormalizedFormula
      }.

    A normalized derivation is an ordinary list of normalized lines. Checking
    proceeds from left to right while carrying the formulas displayed by the
    accepted prefix:

      prefix = []
         |
         | check line 0
         v
      prefix = [formula 0]
         |
         | check line 1
         v
      prefix = [formula 0; formula 1]
         |
         | ...

    The local checker recognizes exactly three rules.

    K:

      A -> (B -> A)

    S:

      (A -> (B -> C)) -> ((A -> B) -> (A -> C))

    MP:

      prefix[p] = A
      prefix[q] = A -> B
      -------------------
             B

    MP citations are prefix lookups, so a line cannot cite itself or a future
    line. The checker does not search for premises: the two indices are part of
    the normalized rule data.

    The sanity layer uses the smallest nontrivial MP derivation. Let

      K  = A -> (B -> A)
      C  = A -> K.

    Then:

      line 0: K                 [K]
      line 1: K -> (A -> K)    [K]
      line 2: A -> K           [MP 0 1]

    `normalized_verifyb` accepts this list for target `C` by direct reduction.
    The general theorem is not the example but the target-sensitive reflection
    result:

      normalized_verifyb lines target = true
        <->
      NormalizedProof lines target.

    Conversely, canonical quotation is complete. Every inductively valid
    normalized proof is encoded and accepted:

      NormalizedProof lines target
        ->
      A002_Verify_certified
        (encode_normalized_derivation lines)
        (encode_normalized_formula target)
      = accept
        (certified_payload
          (encode_normalized_derivation lines)
          (encode_normalized_formula target)).

    `NormalizedLines` is defined independently. Its cons constructor requires
    a valid `NormalizedStep` for the head line and recursively validates the
    tail under the prefix extended by the displayed formula. Thus the Boolean
    checker is proved sound and complete for the inductive rule system.
    `NormalizedProof` additionally requires a nonempty final conclusion equal
    to the requested target.

    Arithmetic normalization supplies the missing transport bridge:

      normalize_derivation : nat -> option NormalizedDerivation
      normalize_formula    : nat -> option NormalizedFormula.

    The public arithmetic verifier is gated by `normalized_verifyb`. Its
    end-to-end theorem states:

      A002_Verify_certified d theta = accept payload
        ->
      exists lines target,
        normalize_derivation d = Some lines /\
        normalize_formula theta = Some target /\
        NormalizedProof lines target.

    `A002_Verify_certified` does not invoke the legacy `A002_Verify`. It makes
    one pass to decode the arithmetic representation into inductive syntax and
    then checks that syntax with `normalized_verifyb`; it does not traverse the
    large arithmetic representation a second time. Clients already holding
    inductive syntax call `normalized_verifyb` directly and incur no A001
    pairing or large-number execution cost.

    The older arithmetic surface represents the same kinds of data with A001
    pairing. A line is

      code_line tag formula = encode tag formula,

    while a tagged cons cell is

      code_cons head tail = encode 1 (encode head tail).

    Repeated pairing makes closed container values grow rapidly. For that
    reason arithmetic codes are retained at transport boundaries, while
    internal effectivity and logical reflection are established on inductive
    normal forms.

    A001 decoding is total on all naturals. A002 therefore never trusts a
    decoded pair merely because decoding produced two projections. A number is
    treated as structured only when

      canonical001b c = true,

    where

      canonical001b c = Nat.eqb (encode (fst001 c) (snd001 c)) c.

    This fixed-point guard prevents malformed arithmetic inputs from being
    silently normalized into valid nodes.


